---
/**
 * Help with the snow app, the same on every site: the support address and short answers. It is
 * the App Store listing's Support URL (willitsnow.today/support), so it is about that app alone
 * and names no other app, site or weather.
 */
import Content from '../layouts/Content.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, type Topic } from '@isithot/core';

interface Props {
  topic: Topic;
}
const { topic } = Astro.props;
---

<Content
  title="Support"
  path="/support"
  description="Help with the willitsnow.today app for iPhone: restoring a purchase, adding a widget, notifications and deleting your data."
  topic={topic}
>
  <h1>Support</h1>
  <p>Help with the willitsnow.today app for iPhone.</p>

  <h2>Contact us</h2>
  <p>
    Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. Tell us what happened, and the
    version number at the end of the app's Settings.
  </p>

  <h2>Restoring a purchase</h2>
  <p>
    The one-off purchase that removes the ads and adds the widgets belongs to your Apple Account. It
    normally comes back by itself on a new iPhone or after you reinstall the app. If it doesn't,
    open Settings in the app (the gear at the top right) and tap "Restore purchase". If no purchase
    is found, check that you're signed in with the Apple Account you bought it with. With Family
    Sharing, your family can restore it the same way.
  </p>

  <h2>Adding a widget</h2>
  <p>
    Widgets come with the one-off purchase, and show the place you chose in the app. On the Home
    Screen, touch and hold an empty part of it until the apps jiggle, tap Edit, then Add Widget, and
    choose the app's Snow forecast widget. On the Lock Screen, touch and hold it, tap Customise,
    then Lock Screen, tap the space below the time and choose Snow forecast.
  </p>

  <h2>If a notification didn't come</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Snow wasn't likely enough.</strong> We send one only when most forecasts expect snow,
      or when some do if you turned on "Also when snow is possible". Forecasts change, so a day that
      looked snowy may no longer be.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It isn't time yet.</strong> The evening notification comes at about 6pm and the
      morning one at about 7am, in the place's own time, not your phone's. Each comes at most once a
      day.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Notifications are off on your iPhone.</strong> Check that they're allowed in your
      iPhone's Settings → Notifications, and that no Focus is holding them back.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It's for another place.</strong> Notifications are for the place in the app's Settings
      → Notifications, not the one on screen. "Use my location" moves it only when you open the app.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The app hasn't been opened for 90 days.</strong> Notifications then stop. Opening the
      app starts them again.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    "Send a test notification" in the app's Settings → Notifications checks that they reach your
    iPhone.
  </p>

  <h2>Turning notifications off and deleting your data</h2>
  <p>
    In the app, open Settings → Your privacy and tap "Turn off notifications and delete my data".
    That turns every notification off and deletes what we keep for them: your device's notification
    token, the place, its time zone, your units and which notifications you chose. The same screen
    shows what we keep while notifications are on. Turning every notification off in Settings →
    Notifications deletes it too. If you delete the app, we delete it when Apple tells us the app is
    no longer installed, or 90 days after you last opened it.
  </p>

  <h2>Privacy and terms</h2>
  <p>
    Read our <a href="/privacy">privacy notice</a>, which covers the app, and our
    <a href="/terms">terms</a>.
  </p>
</Content>
